{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjXqb2imEmG9ubrWTFQw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R445gRCzshMw2aMc6AzpAD","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start of line, after clef and 3 flats: four slurred 16ths, Eb4 F4 D4 Eb4. Then a C4 quarter and a wavy quarter-rest sign. That is bar 36 (Eb4 F4 D4 Eb4, C4 quarter, rest quarter). Barline. Next: 8th rest, then slurred D4 Eb4 F4 G4 and another G4. That is bar 37. Barline. G4 dotted quarter with 'cres.' under it, then slurred F4 Eb4 D4 8ths. That is bar 38. Barline. C4 dotted quarter, D4 and Eb4 16ths, then slurred F4 and F#4 8ths. That is bar 39. Barline. G4 8th slurred to G3 8th, another G3 8th, then the double bar with repeat dots. That is the first half of bar 40, up to the repeat sign. The rest of bar 40 (the 8th rest and the Bb4 quarter) must open the next line. So the line begins at the start of bar 36 and ends partway through bar 40. Nothing contradicts the edition.","right":true,"insights":"This line was easy to match. Its first and last bars have distinctive figures: the 16th-note group falling to a low C, and the octave drop G4–G3 right before the repeat sign. The copyist writes the quarter rest as a wavy stroke and puts the repeat in the middle of bar 40, which could cause confusion. Having the edition mark where the repeat sign falls inside bar 40 made the partial bar easy to confirm."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5416,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5416},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b4602f8-45f9-4b92-a1a6-21e7d4acd3f6","uuid":"785d7d43-8abe